    "title": "The Neolithic Revolution",
    "category": "history",
    "subject": "World History",
    "topic": "The origins of farming",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the beginnings of farming and what changed because of it. Explain causes and consequences in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The Neolithic Revolution is the shift from foraging, living by hunting and gathering, to farming and herding. It began roughly 11,500 years ago, around 9500 BCE, in the Fertile Crescent of Southwest Asia, an arc running from the Levant through southeastern Anatolia to the Zagros Mountains. There people domesticated wheat, barley, lentils, sheep, goats, cattle and pigs.",
      "Farming was not invented once and then spread everywhere. It arose independently in several regions: rice along the Yangtze and millet along the Yellow River in China, maize, beans and squash in Mesoamerica, potatoes and quinoa in the Andes, taro and bananas in New Guinea, and sorghum and other crops in sub-Saharan Africa. Domestication was gradual, taking centuries to millennia, as people selected plants and animals with useful traits.",
      "Farming let people live in permanent settlements and feed more people from the same land, so populations grew. Early villages such as Jericho and Catalhoyuk held hundreds to a few thousand people. Stored surpluses allowed some people to specialize as craftworkers, priests or leaders, and over time societies became more unequal, with property, inheritance and, later, cities and states.",
      "The change had costs. Skeletons of early farmers often show shorter stature, more tooth decay and more signs of malnutrition than those of foragers, because diets narrowed to a few crops and crop failures could cause famine. Living close together and near animals spread infectious diseases. Historians therefore debate whether farming improved individual lives, even though it supported far larger populations."
    ],
