    "title": "The Qin and Han Unification of China",
    "category": "history",
    "subject": "World History",
    "topic": "Early imperial China",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about how China was unified under the Qin and Han dynasties. Explain what each dynasty did and why it mattered, in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "During the Warring States period (475-221 BCE), rival kingdoms fought for control of China. In 221 BCE the state of Qin conquered the last of its rivals, and its king took the new title Shi Huangdi, First Emperor. Qin governed by Legalism, a philosophy of strict laws, harsh punishments and rewards for service to the state.",
      "The Qin replaced hereditary lords with commanderies and counties run by officials the emperor appointed and could remove. It standardized the written script, weights and measures, coinage and even axle widths, built roads and canals, and linked earlier frontier walls into a long northern defense. It also suppressed dissent, ordering in 213 BCE that many books outside approved subjects be burned. Heavy taxes and forced labor bred resentment, and after the First Emperor died in 210 BCE the dynasty collapsed within a few years.",
      "Liu Bang, a commoner who rose in the rebellions, founded the Han dynasty in 202 BCE. The Han kept the Qin's centralized system of officials but softened its laws and lowered taxes. Under Emperor Wu (ruled 141-87 BCE), Confucian teaching became the official learning of the state, and an imperial academy trained officials in the Confucian classics, an early step toward choosing officials for learning rather than birth.",
      "Emperor Wu also expanded Han power. He sent the envoy Zhang Qian to Central Asia, and the contacts that followed helped open the overland trade routes later called the Silk Roads. The Han lasted, with a brief interruption under Wang Mang (9-23 CE), until 220 CE. Its model of a unified empire run by a trained bureaucracy shaped Chinese government for two thousand years, and the majority ethnic group in China still calls itself Han."
    ],
