    "title": "The Fall of the Roman Republic",
    "category": "history",
    "subject": "World History",
    "topic": "Ancient Rome",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about how the Roman Republic gave way to rule by one man. Explain the causes and turning points in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "By the second century BCE the Roman Republic ruled the Mediterranean, but conquest strained it. Wealthy families bought up land and worked it with enslaved captives, while many small farmers who served as soldiers lost their farms. In 133 BCE the tribune Tiberius Gracchus proposed redistributing public land to the poor; he was killed by a group of senators and their supporters. His brother Gaius met a similar end in 121 BCE. Political violence had entered Roman politics.",
      "Gaius Marius, consul several times from 107 BCE, opened the legions to landless volunteers. Soldiers now looked to their generals for pay and land when they retired, so armies grew loyal to commanders rather than to the Senate. In 88 BCE Sulla marched his army on Rome itself, and he later ruled as dictator, purging his enemies through proscription lists.",
      "In 60 BCE Julius Caesar, Pompey and Crassus formed an informal alliance, the First Triumvirate, to get around the Senate. Caesar's conquest of Gaul gave him a rich, loyal army. When the Senate ordered him to give up command, he crossed the Rubicon River into Italy with his troops in 49 BCE, starting a civil war. He won, was named dictator for life, and was assassinated by senators on the Ides of March, 44 BCE.",
      "Caesar's death did not restore the Republic. His adopted heir Octavian allied with Mark Antony and Lepidus, then defeated Antony and Cleopatra at Actium in 31 BCE. In 27 BCE the Senate gave him the name Augustus. He kept the Senate, consuls and elections in form while holding real power through the army and his offices, calling himself princeps, first citizen. Historians date the Roman Empire from his rule."
    ],
