    "title": "The Mali Empire and Trans-Saharan Trade",
    "category": "history",
    "subject": "World History",
    "topic": "West African empires",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the Mali Empire and the trade that made it rich. Explain how trade, rulers and religion were connected, in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The Mali Empire grew in the upper Niger River region of West Africa in the thirteenth century. According to oral tradition preserved by griots, the hereditary storytellers and historians of the Mande peoples, its founder Sundiata Keita defeated the Sosso king Sumanguru at the battle of Kirina around 1235. At its height Mali stretched from the Atlantic coast deep into the interior, larger than any earlier West African state.",
      "Mali's wealth came from trans-Saharan trade. West Africa had rich goldfields, while the Sahara had salt, which was scarce in the farming lands to the south. Camel caravans, organized largely by North African and Saharan merchants, crossed the desert carrying salt, cloth, copper and horses south and gold, ivory and enslaved people north. Mali's rulers taxed this trade and controlled the routes and market towns.",
      "Islam came to West Africa mainly through merchants. Mali's rulers and many townspeople became Muslim, which eased ties with North African traders and brought literate scholars into government. Many rural people kept older religious practices, and rulers often blended Islamic and traditional roles. Timbuktu grew into a center of trade and Islamic scholarship, with mosques and libraries of manuscripts.",
      "The best-known ruler, Mansa Musa (ruled about 1312-1337), made the pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324. Arabic writers reported that he spent and gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value there fell for years. His journey made Mali famous in the Mediterranean world, and a 1375 European map shows him holding a gold nugget. Mali declined in the fifteenth century as provinces broke away and the Songhay Empire rose. Historians reconstruct its past from Arabic accounts, such as that of Ibn Battuta, who visited in 1352-1353, from griot traditions and from archaeology."
    ],
