    "title": "The Mongol Empire",
    "category": "history",
    "subject": "World History",
    "topic": "Eurasian empires",
    "instructions": "The examiner will talk with you about the rise and impact of the Mongol Empire. Explain both its destruction and its connections across Eurasia, in your own words. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "In 1206 an assembly of steppe leaders proclaimed the Mongol chief Temujin as Chinggis (Genghis) Khan, ruler of the peoples of the Mongolian steppe. He reorganized his army into units of ten, a hundred, a thousand and ten thousand, mixing men from different tribes to weaken old loyalties and promoting commanders for ability and loyalty rather than birth. Mongol armies were mounted archers, highly mobile, and well coordinated.",
      "Chinggis Khan and his successors built the largest land empire in history. They conquered northern China, Central Asia, Persia and the Rus principalities, and raided into Poland and Hungary in 1241. In 1258 Hulegu sacked Baghdad and ended the Abbasid Caliphate. Cities that resisted were often destroyed and their people massacred, and the conquests killed very large numbers of people. The Mongol advance into the Middle East was stopped by the Mamluks of Egypt at Ain Jalut in 1260.",
      "By the 1260s the empire had divided into four khanates: the Yuan dynasty in China, founded by Kublai Khan, who completed the conquest of the Song in 1279; the Golden Horde on the western steppe and Rus; the Ilkhanate in Persia; and the Chagatai Khanate in Central Asia. Mongol rulers often adopted the religions and administrative practices of the people they ruled: the Ilkhans became Muslim, and the Yuan used Chinese forms of government.",
      "Once conquest ended, Mongol rule linked Eurasia. A relay system of post stations, the yam, carried messages and officials quickly across vast distances, and protected routes helped merchants, missionaries and envoys travel, such as the Venetian Marco Polo. Mongol rulers generally tolerated many religions. Historians call this period the Pax Mongolica. The same connections probably helped the Black Death spread from Central Asia toward the Mediterranean in the 1340s."
    ],
