      "In 1853 the American commodore Matthew Perry arrived with steam-powered warships and demanded that Japan open its ports. The shogunate signed the Treaty of Kanagawa in 1854, and in 1858 agreed to treaties with the United States and European powers that fixed low tariffs and gave foreigners extraterritoriality, the right to be tried under their own laws. Many Japanese saw these unequal treaties as humiliating, and the shogunate's weakness fed opposition.",
      "Young samurai from the domains of Satsuma and Choshu led the movement against the shogunate under the slogan of restoring the emperor's rule. In late 1867 the last shogun, Tokugawa Yoshinobu, resigned, and in January 1868 imperial rule was proclaimed in the name of the young Emperor Meiji. The Boshin War of 1868-1869 defeated the shogunate's remaining supporters. Real power lay with a small group of oligarchs who governed in the emperor's name.",
      "The new government transformed Japan within a generation. It abolished the domains and created prefectures in 1871, ended the samurai's privileges, and introduced universal male conscription in 1873. It built railways, telegraphs and state-backed industries, created a national school system, and sent the Iwakura Mission (1871-1873) to study Western countries. The Meiji Constitution of 1889, modeled partly on Prussia's, created a parliament with limited powers. Japan adopted Western techniques selectively while promoting loyalty to the emperor, and its victories over China in 1895 and Russia in 1905 made it a major power. By 1911 it had ended the unequal treaties."
