      "By 1914 Europe's great powers were divided into two blocs. Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy formed the Triple Alliance of 1882. France and Russia were allied from 1894, and Britain, though not formally bound, drew close to them through the Entente Cordiale with France in 1904 and an agreement with Russia in 1907, forming the Triple Entente. These arrangements meant a local conflict could quickly involve several powers.",
      "Other long-term tensions added to the danger. Militarism showed in growing armies, detailed mobilization plans and an Anglo-German naval race after Britain launched HMS Dreadnought in 1906. Imperial rivalries outside Europe produced crises over Morocco in 1905 and 1911. Nationalism threatened multiethnic empires: Serbia hoped to unite South Slavs, many of whom lived in Austria-Hungary, which had annexed Bosnia in 1908. The Balkan Wars of 1912-1913 left the region unstable.",
      "On 28 June 1914 Gavrilo Princip, a Bosnian Serb nationalist linked to a secret Serbian group called the Black Hand, assassinated Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo. Germany assured Austria-Hungary of its support, the so-called blank cheque. Austria-Hungary sent Serbia a harsh ultimatum on 23 July and declared war on 28 July. Russia ordered general mobilization in support of Serbia, Germany declared war on Russia on 1 August and on France on 3 August, and German troops invaded neutral Belgium under the Schlieffen Plan. Britain declared war on Germany on 4 August, citing Belgian neutrality. Italy stayed neutral and joined the Entente in 1915.",
      "Who bears responsibility remains debated. The Treaty of Versailles of 1919 placed responsibility on Germany and its allies. In the 1960s the historian Fritz Fischer argued that Germany's leaders pursued aggressive aims, while others stress shared responsibility; Christopher Clark's The Sleepwalkers (2012) argues that leaders in several capitals made choices that together brought war. Most historians agree that no single cause explains the war: long-term tensions made it possible, and decisions in the July Crisis made it happen."
